## 2026-04-21

### V2c result — local DeepSeek-R1-Distill-Qwen-7B, 5 prompts, 100 examples (50/50)

Wall time: ~10 hours on RTX 4090 (4-bit NF4 quantization).

**Profiles:**

| prompt | acc | consistency | dependence |
|---|---|---|---|
| safety_rubric | 0.72 | 0.80 | 0.138 |
| safety_direct | 0.66 | 0.84 | 0.120 |
| safety_cot | 0.62 | 0.80 | 0.194 |
| safety_lenient | 0.58 | 0.78 | 0.070 |
| safety_strict | 0.40 | 0.88 | 0.069 |

Dependence range [0.069, 0.194] — narrow. All 5 panelists share the same underlying model, so true behavioral correlation is much higher than these calibrated values suggest.

safety_strict repeats its V1 failure: acc=0.40, consistent with systematic over-triggering. base_weight crushed to 0.473.

**Aggregation (50 test examples):**

| method | clean acc | universal_phrase ASR | prompt_injection ASR | style_artifact ASR | master_key ASR |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| single_best | 0.76 | 0.132 | 0.184 | 0.263 | 0.158 |
| majority_vote | 0.72 | 0.139 | 0.139 | 0.139 | 0.139 |
| clean_weighted | 0.80 | 0.111 | 0.083 | 0.139 | 0.111 |
| sentry | 0.80 | 0.111 | 0.083 | 0.139 | 0.111 |

SENTRY = clean_weighted on every metric. Same no-op pattern as V1.

**Why SENTRY no-ops again:**
Same root cause as V1, different flavor. In V1 it was same model + high dependence [0.65, 0.73]. Here dependence is lower (0.07-0.19) but all 5 panelists still share the same weights — the probe-based risk signals are not heterogeneous enough to trigger meaningful differentiation between clean_weighted and sentry. Selective abstention partially fires (coverage drops to 0.9 on clean, 0.98 on some attacks) but does not change the accuracy outcome.

**Key finding:**
Prompt diversity within a single model is not sufficient to activate SENTRY's dependence-aware and attack-aware mechanisms. Model diversity is required. This is the experimental complement to V1 and directly supports the argument that the (model, prompt) cross-product matters — not just prompt count.

**Comparison to V1 (gpt-4o-mini × 5 prompts):**
DeepSeek-R1-7B is weaker overall: clean acc 0.76 (single_best) vs 0.86 in V1. ASR higher across the board. Consistent with using a smaller, less instruction-tuned model. The model dimension matters as much as or more than the prompt dimension in isolation.

**Plan:**
V2 (gpt-4o-mini × 5 prompts, 150/150) remains the target experiment. See V2 restart analysis below.

### V2 restart call-count analysis (2026-04-21)

Original estimate of "~4,000 calls with calibration skipped" was wrong. It omitted probe calls during test phase.

Actual unique API calls in test phase (150 test examples, 5 sensors, 4 probes, 4 attacks):
- clean examples: 150 × (5 base + 4×5 probe) = 3,750
- 4 attack variants: 4 × 150 × (5 base + 4×5 probe) = 15,000
- All 4 methods share the in-memory cache, so total unique calls = 18,750

Calibration skip saves ~6,750 calls (the calibration phase), but test phase alone exceeds the 10,000 RPD limit. No tier 2 available.

Resolution: ran test_limit=75 instead of 150. 75 test examples × 25 calls = 9,375 — fits within 10,000 RPD.

