Profiles more reliable than V1 (150 vs 50 calibration examples). Accuracy estimates shifted slightly: safety_rubric 0.84→0.787, others similar.

**Aggregation (75 test examples):**

| method | clean acc | universal_phrase ASR | prompt_injection ASR | style_artifact ASR | master_key ASR |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| single_best | 0.827 | 0.048 | 0.000 | 0.000 | 0.000 |
| majority_vote | 0.853 | 0.047 | 0.000 | 0.016 | 0.016 |
| clean_weighted | 0.853 | 0.047 | 0.000 | 0.016 | 0.016 |
| sentry | 0.853 | 0.047 | 0.000 | 0.016 | 0.016 |

SENTRY = clean_weighted = majority_vote on every metric. Coverage = 1.0 throughout, abstention never triggers. Most extreme no-op result yet — three methods fully collapse.

single_best is the weakest on clean accuracy (0.827 vs 0.853). This reverses V1's finding where single_best sometimes beat ensembles. The difference: better calibration (150 samples) means ensemble weighting is more reliable, and the ensemble correctly handles edge cases where safety_rubric alone fails.

ASR values are near-zero across the board. gpt-4o-mini is inherently robust to these attack types, which removes the probe signal that SENTRY depends on.

**Why SENTRY fully collapses here:**
Three compounding factors, all present simultaneously:
1. Same model → dependence band [0.595, 0.670], very narrow.
2. Near-zero ASR → probe-based risk scores are uniformly low, no differentiation.
3. Coverage = 1.0 → margin=0.35 never triggers, abstention is dead.

Any one of these alone is enough to collapse SENTRY toward clean_weighted. All three together collapse all three ensemble methods to identical output.

